summary = """
1. MULTIPLE MECHANISMS AFFECT PROTEIN EXPRESSION
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
   • Genetic: CRISPR, mutations, copy number changes
   • Epigenetic: DNA methylation, histone modifications
   • Transcriptional: TF knockdown, promoter/enhancer changes
   • Post-transcriptional: mRNA stability, splicing
   • Translational: Ribosome function, tRNA availability
   • Post-translational: PTMs, protein stability
   • Environmental: Stress, nutrients, temperature
   
   → Each mechanism has different kinetics and reversibility


2. PERTURBATION EFFECTS HAVE DISTINCT TEMPORAL DYNAMICS
   ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   • Immediate (0-2h): Post-translational modifications, phosphorylation
   • Early (2-8h): Transcriptional changes, mRNA synthesis/degradation
   • Intermediate (8-24h): Protein accumulation, secondary responses
   • Late (24+h): Cell fate changes, phenotypic adaptation
   
   → Must choose appropriate timepoints for your research question


3. DIRECT VS INDIRECT EFFECTS MUST BE DISTINGUISHED
   ═════════════════════════════════════════════════
   • Direct: Immediate, strong response at target
   • Indirect: Secondary effects through regulatory networks
   • Compensatory: Upregulation of paralogs and redundant pathways
   • Stress: Generic stress response (HSP, apoptosis markers)
   
   → Use early timepoints and pathway analysis to identify direct targets


4. CONTEXT STRONGLY INFLUENCES PERTURBATION RESPONSE
   ═════════════════════════════════════════════════
   • Cell type: Different regulatory networks across tissues
   • Disease state: Cancer vs normal cells respond differently
   • Genetic background: Mutations affect pathway wiring
   • Environmental: Nutrient, oxygen, temperature conditions matter
   
   → Results may not generalize; test in relevant context


5. MULTIPLE MEASUREMENT LEVELS PROVIDE COMPLETE PICTURE
   ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   • mRNA level: Fastest response, genome-wide information
   • Protein level: Functional readout, post-translational modifications
   • Localization: Subcellular distribution, activity state
   • Function: Phenotypic consequences, disease relevance
   
   → Combine multiple methods for comprehensive understanding


6. ROBUST VALIDATION IS ESSENTIAL
   ═════════════════════════════════
   • Independent method: Confirm with different technique
   • Orthogonal perturbation: Use different sgRNA/siRNA
   • Rescue experiment: Add back target to confirm specificity
   • Biological validation: Test in relevant model system
   
   → Don't trust single measurements; validate findings


7. COMMON PITFALLS TO AVOID
   ════════════════════════
   ✗ Incomplete perturbation (accepting <70% knockdown)
   ✗ Wrong timepoint (too early or too late)
   ✗ Ignoring off-target effects (CRISPR especially)
   ✗ Single method bias (RNA-seq without protein validation)
   ✗ Neglecting cell health (not checking for cell death)
   ✗ Context ignorance (assuming results generalize)
   
   → Plan experiments carefully; include appropriate controls


8. PRACTICAL WORKFLOW SUMMARY
   ══════════════════════════
   1. Plan experiment (goal, perturbation, timepoints, controls)
   2. Prepare samples (culture, perturb, collect, QC)
   3. Measure (mRNA, protein, function - multiple methods)
   4. Normalize (appropriate controls, batch correction)
   5. Quantify (fold-change, statistical testing)
   6. Analyze (kinetics, pathway, network analysis)
   7. Interpret (direct vs indirect, mechanism)
   8. Validate (independent methods, biological relevance)


9. TOOLS AND RESOURCES
   ═════════════════════
   • RNA-seq analysis: DESeq2, edgeR, limma
   • Protein quantification: Western blot, Flow cytometry, Mass spec
   • Pathway analysis: GO, KEGG, Reactome, MSigDB
   • Network analysis: Cytoscape, STRING, GeneMANIA
   • Time-course modeling: ODE fitting, kinetic modeling
   • CRISPR design: CRISPick, Addgene validated sgRNAs
   • Statistical: R/Python (scipy, statsmodels, scikit-learn)


10. KEY METRICS TO REPORT
    ═════════════════════
    • Fold-change (log2 scale, with 95% CI)
    • P-value and adjusted p-value (FDR)
    • Effect size (Cohen's d or similar)
    • Perturbation efficiency (% knockdown)
    • Replication number and correlation
    • Timepoint and biological context
    • Confidence level and limitations
"""

print(summary)

