checklist = """
BEFORE STARTING YOUR EXPERIMENT
═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Project Planning
□ Define primary research question
□ Select perturbation method (CRISPR, RNAi, drug, environmental)
□ Choose appropriate cell type/tissue
□ Document expected baseline expression levels
□ Identify known markers for your target
□ Review literature for similar studies

Experimental Design
□ Plan time course (early, intermediate, late timepoints)
□ Design control samples (negative, positive, mock)
□ Calculate required sample size (minimum 3 replicates, recommend 4-6)
□ Prepare reagent list with catalog numbers
□ Establish quality control criteria
□ Create detailed protocol document
□ Estimate budget and timeline

═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

DURING EXPERIMENT
═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Perturbation Application
□ Verify cell culture conditions (density, viability >95%)
□ Prepare perturbation reagent (verify concentration/titer)
□ Apply perturbation at consistent time (T=0)
□ Include all planned controls
□ Document exact timing for each sample
□ Maintain consistent culture conditions throughout

Quality Control During Experiment
□ Monitor cell morphology at each timepoint
□ Check for unexpected cell death
□ Verify culture conditions (temperature, CO2, etc.)
□ Document any deviations from protocol
□ Take photos of culture plates
□ Record exact collection times

Sample Collection
□ Use consistent lysis buffer/method
□ Collect samples at all planned timepoints
□ Include controls in every batch
□ Snap freeze samples if storing
□ Label samples clearly with date, time, condition
□ Maintain sample integrity (avoid freeze-thaw cycles)

═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

AFTER EXPERIMENT - QUALITY CONTROL
═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Verify Perturbation
□ Confirm target knockdown (>70% for functional studies)
□ Check for off-target effects (validate with >1 sgRNA if CRISPR)
□ Assess cell viability (>80% typically required)
□ Document any incomplete penetrance
□ Validate with independent method if possible

Assess Data Quality
□ Check RNA integrity (RIN >7 for RNA-seq)
□ Verify protein concentration consistency
□ Assess sequencing quality (Q30 >80% for RNA-seq)
□ Calculate replicate correlation (R² >0.8 expected)
□ Identify and document any outliers
□ Perform PCA to check for batch effects

═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

DATA ANALYSIS
═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Normalization
□ Use appropriate normalization method (DESeq2, CPM, etc.)
□ Select stable housekeeping genes as reference
□ Identify and correct batch effects
□ Create PCA plot to visualize data quality
□ Document all normalization parameters
□ Check for technical artifacts

Quantification
□ Calculate fold-change for all genes
□ Perform statistical testing (t-test, ANOVA)
□ Adjust p-values for multiple comparisons
□ Report effect sizes (Cohen's d or similar)
□ Create volcano plot for visualization
□ List top differentially expressed genes

Temporal Analysis
□ Plot expression over time for key genes
□ Identify response kinetics (immediate, early, late)
□ Fit kinetic models if applicable
□ Calculate lag time and steady-state levels
□ Cluster genes by response pattern
□ Identify genes with similar dynamics

Pathway and Network Analysis
□ Perform GO enrichment on upregulated genes
□ Perform GO enrichment on downregulated genes
□ Map to known pathways (KEGG, Reactome)
□ Identify pathway modules and hub genes
□ Compare to disease signatures
□ Create pathway diagrams

═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

INTERPRETATION AND VALIDATION
═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Distinguish Effect Types
□ Identify direct targets (immediate, strong response)
□ Identify primary indirect effects (early secondary response)
□ Identify secondary effects (feedback loops, compensation)
□ Check for stress response markers
□ Document confidence level for each finding

Biological Interpretation
□ Compare to known disease signatures
□ Link molecular changes to phenotype
□ Assess functional consequences
□ Consider biological plausibility
□ Note any unexpected findings
□ Suggest mechanisms for further investigation

Validation
□ Confirm key findings with independent method
□ Validate ≥3 differentially expressed genes
□ Test rescue experiment (add back target)
□ Test in different cell type if applicable
□ Compare to published studies
□ Note any conflicting results

═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

REPORTING
═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Methods Section
□ Describe perturbation method and reagents (catalog numbers)
□ Document cell culture conditions
□ List all timepoints and replicates
□ Describe measurement methods
□ Explain normalization and statistical approach
□ Note any deviations from standard protocols

Results Section
□ Report fold-changes with confidence intervals
□ Include p-values and adjusted p-values
□ Describe effect sizes
□ Document perturbation efficiency
□ Present heatmaps/volcano plots
□ Summarize pathway analysis results

Discussion
□ Interpret direct vs indirect effects
□ Compare to literature findings
□ Discuss limitations and caveats
□ Suggest follow-up experiments
□ Link to biological significance
□ Address any unexpected findings

Supplementary Data
□ Provide complete gene lists (all tested genes)
□ Include pathway enrichment results
□ Document QC metrics
□ Provide raw data (if appropriate)
□ List all statistical parameters used

═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
"""

